---
// Method sheet for "The State Before the Interface". English-only. Deliberately free of
// live counts: the observatory's own register derives its figures from committed records;
// this sheet states only dated facts and standing rules that do not drift.
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'

const REGISTER = 'https://frankbueltge.github.io/state-before-interface/'
const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/state-before-interface'
---

<Page
  title="Method sheet — The State Before the Interface | Frank Bültge"
  description="How the observatory is made: TED Search API v3 as the single source, a nightly deterministic sentinel, a six-criteria gate, provenance invariants enforced by a verifier, and autonomy measured step by step."
>
  <main id="main" class="flex-1">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-14 font-mono">
    <p class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-fg-faint">Method sheet</p>
    <h1 class="mb-8 font-sans text-2xl font-semibold">The State Before the Interface</h1>

    <section class="mb-10 border border-line bg-panel/60 p-5">
      <h2 class="mb-2 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">What this is</h2>
      <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        An autonomous observatory of public AI procurement in Europe, run as one machine in one
        public repository. It tests a research question, not a product: can the chain
        signal → candidate → investigation → evidence → verification → form → public result run
        on its own — and what does that autonomy cost, where does it fail, who has to step in?
        It is deliberately not part of the research ecology; it is the counter-experiment.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="space-y-9 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">1. Source &amp; provenance</h2>
        <p>
          One source in V0: the EU's Tenders Electronic Daily via the public
          <a href="https://docs.ted.europa.eu/api/latest/index.html" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">TED Search API v3</a>
          (anonymous, no key; measured live on 2026-08-08 before any adapter was built). Every
          matching notice is preserved as original eForms-UBL XML; each preserved file carries
          source URL, UTC retrieval time and SHA-256 in a committed manifest. Procurement
          notices are freely reusable documents of the Publications Office of the EU. The
          machine's own derived data is CC0, texts CC BY 4.0, code Apache-2.0 — all in
          <a href={REPO} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">the archive</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">2. Cadence</h2>
        <p>
          One sentinel run per night (05:15 UTC, GitHub Actions), committed under the machine's
          own identity. Git is the archive: snapshots are append-only, closed cases are never
          edited, corrections supersede rather than overwrite, and there is no backfill — a
          night the source was down is recorded as exactly that. A night without signal is the
          normal case and is committed as "no signal".
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">3. Processing</h2>
        <p>
          The sentinel is deterministic — no model touches observation, preservation, diffing or
          the gate. It searches a versioned multilingual envelope (v1: the exact phrases
          "artificial intelligence", "Künstliche Intelligenz", "intelligence artificielle"),
          detects four difference types (new match, changed record, disappeared record,
          cross-border vendor pattern) and hands each signal to a six-criteria gate whose
          verdict is committed with per-criterion reasons. Case mode — the investigation of a
          surviving candidate — is an ephemeral, fully attributed agent run that delivers by
          pull request; a provenance verifier walks every published claim back to hashed bytes
          and fails the build on any hole. The public register is a deterministic rebuild of the
          committed records; the verifier fails if the two differ by one byte.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">4. Limits, named</h2>
        <p>
          Full-text search has bycatch by design (a signal generator mentioning "artificial
          intelligence" in passing was measured on day one) — the gate, not the envelope, sorts
          it out, and a declined candidate stays on the record. The envelope sees only three
          languages so far; coverage grows by committed, reasoned revisions. TED's full-text
          hit series before 2024 is treated as uncalibrated (a suspected eForms indexing
          artifact around 2022/23) and is not used for historical claims. The EU's public
          register of high-risk AI systems was deferred to 2027-12-02 (Digital Omnibus), so the
          two-register comparison is a baseline mission until then. Derived data carries
          organization-level fields only — natural persons never leave the raw record. Costs
          and models are logged per step; no aggregate autonomy score exists, by design.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold text-fg">5. Verify it yourself</h2>
        <p>
          Clone <a href={REPO} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">the repository</a>, then:
          <code class="text-fg-muted">python verify.py --repo-root .</code> walks the whole
          chain backwards; <code class="text-fg-muted">cd sentinel &amp;&amp; python -m pytest</code>
          runs the invariants as tests. The
          <a href={REGISTER} class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4">register</a>
          links every figure to the committed record it is derived from.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
  </main>
</Page>
